# Stop Dev Server On Port 8020

The developer owns `pnpm run dev`. The agent must **not** leave a Nest process listening on **8020**.

## When

After completing a feature, bug fix, refactor, or any work session that may have started/restarted the API (including watch mode / failed boot leaving a listener):

1. Check whether something listens on port **8020**.
2. If yes, **stop that process** before reporting the task done.
3. Do **not** start or restart the server afterward — the user will run it.

## How (Windows PowerShell)

```powershell
$conns = @(Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8020 -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object -ExpandProperty OwningProcess -Unique)
foreach ($procId in $conns) { Stop-Process -Id $procId -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue }
```

Confirm the port is free. Prefer a variable name other than `$pid` (read-only in PowerShell).

## Anti-patterns

- Leaving `pnpm run dev` running in the background after the task
- Assuming a crash freed the port (`EADDRINUSE` means it did not)
- Starting the server “to verify” and leaving it up
